# CADDY_TLS_MODE=local: Caddy sits behind a proxy that terminates TLS (Nginx
# Proxy Manager, Traefik, a cloud load balancer), so Let's Encrypt can never
# reach it to validate a certificate. Every site — the domain, its www/
# bookings/dashboard redirect hosts and the meeting host — is issued a
# certificate from Caddy's internal CA instead, and the proxy is configured to
# accept a certificate that is not publicly trusted (Nginx Proxy Manager:
# forward scheme https, port 443, "Ignore Invalid SSL" on). The browser only
# ever sees the proxy's own certificate.
local_certs

# The proxy in front is the only peer Caddy sees, so without this every
# visitor would reach the app as the proxy's address: the per-IP rate limits on
# login, register, forgot-password and magic links would share ONE bucket for
# the whole site and audit-log IPs would all be the proxy. `trusted_proxies`
# makes Caddy read the visitor's address from the proxy's X-Forwarded-For,
# which `{client_ip}` then carries to the app (see `header_up` in
# deploy/caddy/tacbookings-active.caddy).
#
# CADDY_TRUSTED_PROXIES is the proxy's own address or CIDR, with no usable
# default: unset, docker-compose.yml supplies a placeholder Caddy rejects, and
# the deploy refuses local mode without it (DEPLOYMENT.md, "Client IPs behind
# the proxy"). `trusted_proxies_strict` reads X-Forwarded-For from the right,
# skipping only addresses on that list, so the address Caddy uses is the one
# the trusted proxy put there. The proxy must append the connecting address to
# X-Forwarded-For or overwrite the header; one that passes the client's header
# through unchanged is not suitable for local mode.
servers {
	trusted_proxies static {$CADDY_TRUSTED_PROXIES}
	trusted_proxies_strict
	client_ip_headers X-Forwarded-For
}
